The code statements that lead to the bug are:
for (i = 0; i < MSM_VFE_IMAGE_MASTERS_NUM; i++) {
    if (status & BUS_IRQ_MASK_0_RDI_RUP(vfe, i))
        vfe_isr_reg_update(vfe, i);
Reason:
The loop uses `MSM_VFE_IMAGE_MASTERS_NUM` (7) as the bound, but `vfe->line[]` is defined with size `VFE_LINE_NUM_MAX` (4). When `i` is 4, 5, or 6, the call `vfe_isr_reg_update(vfe, i)` accesses `vfe->line[i]`, which exceeds the array bounds, causing an out‑of‑bounds memory access.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
for (i = 0; i < MAX_VFE_OUTPUT_LINES; i++) {
    if (status & BUS_IRQ_MASK_0_RDI_RUP(vfe, i))
        vfe_isr_reg_update(vfe, i);
}
for (i = 0; i < MSM_VFE_IMAGE_MASTERS_NUM; i++) {
    if (status & BUS_IRQ_MASK_0_COMP_DONE(vfe, RDI_COMP_GROUP(i)))
        vfe_buf_done(vfe, i);
}
Reason:
The fix separates the handling of RDI “update” interrupts (which directly correspond to output lines) from the WM “done” interrupts. The first loop now uses the correct bound `MAX_VFE_OUTPUT_LINES` (likely 4) for the `vfe_isr_reg_update()` calls, preventing out‑of‑bounds access to `vfe->line[]`. The second loop retains the original `MSM_VFE_IMAGE_MASTERS_NUM` bound for `vfe_buf_done()` because that function operates on the write master array, which has a different size.

